WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.230
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:10.080
I hope you like wiggles and jiggles, because
today… we are going to a see a lot of them.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.219
You see, this technique promises to imbue
a rigged animation with elastoplastic secondary

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:17.219
effects.

00:00:17.219 --> 00:00:21.660
Now, if you tell this to a computer graphics
researcher, they will be extremely happy to

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:26.340
hear that this is finally possible, but what
does that really mean?

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:31.770
This beautiful fish animation is one of the
finest demonstrations of the new method.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.789
So, what happened here?

00:00:33.789 --> 00:00:39.129
Well, the first part means that we modeled
a piece of 3D geometry, and we wish to make

00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:45.690
it move, but in order to make this movement
believable, we have to specify where the bones

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:48.820
and joints are located within the model.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:53.370
This process is called rigging, and this model
will be the input for it.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:57.710
We can make it move with traditional methods,
well, kind of.

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:03.329
You see that the bones and joints are working,
but the model is still solid.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:08.120
For instance, look…the trunk and ears are
both completely solid.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:11.600
This is not what we would expect to see from
this kind of motion.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.590
So, can we do better than this?

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:19.640
Well, hold on to your papers, and let’s
see how this new technique enhances these

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:20.640
animations.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:26.670
Oh yes, floppy ears, also, the trunk is dangling
everywhere.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.550
What a lovely animation!

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:31.260
Prepare for a lot more wiggles and jiggles.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:36.880
Now, I love how we can set up the material
properties for this method, of course, it

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.590
cannot just make decisions by itself because
it would compete with the artist’s vision,

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:47.190
the goal is always to enhance these animations
in a way that gives artists more control over

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.710
what happens.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:51.540
So what about more elaborate models?

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:52.590
Do they work too?

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:55.740
Let’s have a look and find out together!

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.300
This is the traditional animation method.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:03.900
No deformation on the belly, nostrils are
not moving too much, so now, let’s see the

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.320
new method.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.730
Look at that.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:13.549
The face, ears, nose, and the mouth now show
elastic movement.

00:02:13.549 --> 00:02:14.760
So cool!

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.499
Even the belly is deforming as the model is
running about.

00:02:18.499 --> 00:02:24.090
So we already see that it can kind of deal
with the forces that are present in the simulation.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:27.150
Let’s give this aspect a closer look.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:32.099
This is the traditional method…we are moving
up and down, up and down.

00:02:32.099 --> 00:02:37.430
Alright, but look at the vectors here, there
is an external force field, or in simpler

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.629
words, the wind is blowing.

00:02:39.629 --> 00:02:43.689
And, unfortunately, not much is really happening
to this model.

00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:49.560
But… when we plug in this new technique,
look, it finally responds to these external

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:50.590
forces.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:53.770
And as a reward, we get more wiggles.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:54.770
Ha-haa!

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:57.150
So, what else can this do?

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.150
A lot more.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:04.370
For instance, it does not require this particular
kind of rig with the bones and joints.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:10.379
This hedgehog was instead, rigged with two
handles, which is much simpler, but unfortunately,

00:03:10.379 --> 00:03:16.029
when we start to move it with traditional
techniques, …well… leg is kind of pinned

00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:19.129
to the ground, where the remainder of the
model moves.

00:03:19.129 --> 00:03:22.459
So, how did the new technique deal with this?

00:03:22.459 --> 00:03:29.010
Oh yes, the animation is much more realistic,
and things are dangling around in a much more

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.219
lively manner.

00:03:30.219 --> 00:03:35.349
The fact that it works on many different kinds
of rigged models out there in a wild bolsters

00:03:35.349 --> 00:03:38.290
the usability of this technique a great deal.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:40.379
But we are not done yet.

00:03:40.379 --> 00:03:41.379
No-no!

00:03:41.379 --> 00:03:46.120
If you have been holding on to your papers
so far, now, squeeze that paper, because we

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:52.109
can take a scene, drop in a bunch of objects,
and expect a realistic output.

00:03:52.109 --> 00:03:57.590
But wait a second…if you have been watching
the series for a while, you know for a fact

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:00.859
that for this, we need to run an elaborate
physics simulator.

00:04:00.859 --> 00:04:04.909
For instance, just look at this muscle simulation
from earlier.

00:04:04.909 --> 00:04:10.169
And here is the key, this animation took over
an hour for every second of video footage

00:04:10.169 --> 00:04:11.409
that you see here.

00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:15.840
The new method does not need to compute a
full-blown physics simulation to add this

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:21.549
kind of elastic behavior, and hence, in many
cases, it runs in real time.

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:26.860
And it works for all kinds of rigs out there
in the wild, and we even have artistic control

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:27.860
over the output.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:32.930
We don’t need elaborate models and many
hours of rigging and simulation to be able

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:38.900
to create a beautiful animation anymore.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:53.750
What 

00:04:53.750 --> 00:05:24.650
a time 

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:38.550
to be alive!

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:41.819
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

